//===- LangFeatures.def - Capabilities more than one dialect has -*- C++ -*-===//
//
// A capability that some dialects have and others do not, named so that the
// code gating it can say which capability it means rather than which standard
// happens to have it.
//
//   FEATURE(Id, Dialects)
//
//     Id        The name the compiler asks for: Opts.has(Feature::Id).
//     Dialects  Which dialects have it, as D_* bits from LangOptions.h.
//
// WHAT BELONGS HERE, AND WHAT DOES NOT
//
// Only a capability *shared* by more than one dialect.  Extended Pascal has
// about thirty extensions over ISO 7185, and most of them -- schema types,
// `type of`, `bindable`, `restricted`, `**`, `><`, `value`, `for ... in`,
// structured value constructors, non-decimal literals, modules -- are its
// alone, and Turbo must never inherit them.  `Vector(10)` in Turbo is a call,
// not a type.  Those sites keep asking `extendedPascal()`, which is exactly
// what they mean, and are not listed here.
//
// Writing them all out as a dialect matrix would mean transcribing an Extended
// Pascal column by hand for thirty rows, and a mistranscribed cell changes
// Extended Pascal silently: both of plang's conformance corpora are ISO 7185
// programs, so an ISO 7185 mode that grew an extension, or an Extended Pascal
// mode that lost one, passes all 377 of them.  The narrower list is the safer
// one.
//
// The pairs in test/Sema/sema_test.cpp's DialectGating suite are what keep
// these honest: one program per capability, accepted under a dialect that has
// it and refused with a named diagnostic under one that does not.  Adding a
// FEATURE here without adding that pair leaves the new cell untested.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

#ifndef FEATURE
#define FEATURE(Id, Dialects)
#endif

// ISO 10206 §6.2.1 / Turbo: declaration sections in any order, and repeated.
// ISO 7185 fixes the order label, const, type, var and allows one of each.
FEATURE(FreeDeclarationOrder,   D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.8.2 / Turbo: any nonvarying expression where ISO 7185 takes only a
// simple-expression, so a relational operator is the boundary and `2 + 3` is
// not: both dialects parse that.
FEATURE(ConstantExpressions,    D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.9.3.5 `otherwise`; Turbo spells the same arm `else`.
FEATURE(CaseDefaultArm,         D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.4.3.4 / Turbo: `1..3:` as a case-constant.
FEATURE(CaseConstantRanges,     D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.4.2.4 / Turbo: a subrange bound may be a constant expression.
FEATURE(SubrangeBoundExprs,     D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// ISO 7185 §6.1.7 wants at least one character in a string literal; EP and
// Turbo both allow none.
FEATURE(EmptyStringLiteral,     D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.1.3 / Turbo: `_` is a letter inside an identifier.
FEATURE(UnderscoreIdentifiers,  D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

// EP §6.8.3.2 / Turbo: two chars concatenate with `+` rather than failing as
// non-numeric operands.  Only char + char: a char with a string, or two
// strings, already concatenate in every dialect, so this one operand pair is
// the whole of the difference.
FEATURE(CharConcatenation,      D_ISO10206 | D_Turbo)

#undef FEATURE
